Marginal Gains for All Humanity

An Empirical Investigation into the Universal Benefit of Things Nobody Asked For

Abstract

A growing number of corporations, laboratories, founders, investors and assorted individuals with access to conference stages have announced that their activities are being undertaken “for humanity.”

This paper investigates humanity’s involvement in this arrangement.

Initial results are concerning.

Despite humanity comprising approximately the entire population of humanity, we find remarkably little evidence that humanity has been consulted regarding what it wants, where it would like to go, what risks it considers acceptable, or whether it wishes to participate in the proposed future at all.

Instead, the dominant methodology appears to consist of a comparatively small number of people deciding what would be good for everyone else, announcing that decision publicly, raising substantial amounts of capital around it, and subsequently interpreting disagreement as either ignorance, fear of progress, insufficient understanding of the technology, or a temporary communications problem.

We call this phenomenon Universal Beneficence by Assertion (UBA).

Under UBA, the statement

“This will benefit humanity.”

is treated not as a hypothesis requiring validation, but as an administrative permission slip issued by the person making the statement.

This paper proposes several new quantitative measures for assessing such claims, including the Humanity Consultation Ratio, the Beneficence-to-Consent Coefficient, and the Marginal Humanity Gain, defined as the additional quantity of humanity allegedly saved per unit reduction in humanity’s ability to object.

Our findings suggest that humanity may indeed possess a future.

Whether it is the future currently being constructed on its behalf remains an open question.

