1. Introduction

Humanity is extremely popular.

It is difficult to attend a technology conference, investment presentation, artificial-intelligence announcement, biotechnology summit or discussion of the distant future without discovering that somebody is working extremely hard on humanity’s behalf.

Humanity will flourish.

Humanity will prosper.

Humanity will become more productive.

Humanity will be transformed.

Humanity will achieve abundance.

Humanity, reassuringly, appears to have an enormous number of unpaid strategic consultants.

What remains less clear is when humanity appointed them.

The problem is not necessarily dishonesty.

Indeed, the more interesting possibility is that many of the people making these claims sincerely believe them.

A person can genuinely believe that the thing they are building will improve the world.

They can work eighteen hours a day on it.

They can sacrifice relationships, money and sleep for it.

They can produce lengthy philosophical arguments explaining why it is necessary.

They can surround themselves with other intelligent people who agree.

They can establish institutes devoted to ensuring that it happens responsibly.

And they can still be wrong.

This presents an awkward problem for the increasingly popular phrase “for humanity.”

The phrase quietly converts an individual preference into a species-level mandate.

“I want to build this” becomes:

“We need this.”

“We need this” becomes:

“Humanity needs this.”

And once humanity itself has apparently entered the meeting and voted in favour, opposition acquires a strange character.

The critic is no longer merely disagreeing with a commercial strategy or technological programme.

The critic is standing in the way of humanity.

This is an extraordinarily useful rhetorical technology.

It may even be more powerful than the technology being advertised.

