2. The Humanity Consultation Problem

Consider a hypothetical organisation developing Technology X.

Its leadership believes Technology X will profoundly improve civilisation.

Let:

[
H = \text{number of humans affected}
]

[
C = \text{number of humans meaningfully consulted}
]

We define the Humanity Consultation Ratio:

[
HCR = \frac{C}{H}
]

For genuinely species-wide claims, we would ideally expect (HCR) to be non-negligible.

In practice, an approximate decision-making structure may instead consist of:

* several founders;
* several investors;
* several researchers;
* a policy team;
* three philosophers;
* an economist;
* somebody who once worked in government;
* and a public-relations department responsible for explaining the resulting decision to the remaining eight billion people.

This produces an (HCR) extremely close to zero.

Remarkably, however, confidence in the statement “this is good for humanity” frequently approaches one.

We therefore observe an apparent inverse relationship:

[
\text{Confidence in knowing humanity’s interests}
\propto
\frac{1}{\text{amount of humanity consulted}}
]

This phenomenon deserves further study.

