3. The Beneficence-to-Consent Coefficient

We next introduce the Beneficence-to-Consent Coefficient (BCC).

Let:

[
B = \text{magnitude of claimed benefit}
]

and

[
K = \text{degree of meaningful public consent}
]

Then:

[
BCC = \frac{B}{K}
]

A curious feature of modern technological rhetoric is that (B) can become arbitrarily large.

A product may initially save time.

Its successor may transform work.

The next iteration may transform society.

Shortly afterwards, the programme may unlock human potential, eliminate scarcity, solve education and usher civilisation into an age of abundance.

Meanwhile (K) can remain essentially unchanged.

Thus the strongest claims about humanity can coexist with almost no involvement from humanity itself.

In ordinary engineering this would be considered a stakeholder-management failure.

At sufficient scale, however, it apparently becomes visionary leadership.

