5. Humanity May Possess Preferences

A major limitation of the existing literature is the assumption that humanity shares a single objective function.

This is unlikely.

Human beings disagree about almost everything.

They disagree about politics, economics, religion, education, privacy, work, technology, risk, leisure, family, property, equality, freedom and whether pineapple belongs on pizza.

It would therefore be surprising if, upon reaching sufficiently advanced technology, eight billion people suddenly converged upon one Silicon Valley product roadmap.

Some people want radical technological acceleration.

Some want strict regulation.

Some want stronger communities.

Some want fewer computers.

Some want more computers.

Some want a four-day week.

Some want to colonise Mars.

Some want to grow tomatoes.

There may even exist a statistically non-zero population who would prefer that civilisation abandon the entire experiment and return to hitting one another with axes and jousting.

Whether this policy would outperform current arrangements is outside the scope of this paper.

The important point is that nobody checked.

