6. The Benevolent Dictator Error

The central philosophical error behind Universal Beneficence by Assertion is remarkably old.

It can be expressed as:

I know what is good for you; therefore I am justified in doing it to you.

The modern version substitutes sufficiently impressive vocabulary:

Our models indicate that this trajectory maximises humanity’s long-term flourishing.

The logical structure remains largely unchanged.

Expertise can tell us what consequences a decision may produce.

It cannot independently determine which consequences everybody ought to prefer.

Engineering can optimise a system after somebody specifies the objective.

Engineering cannot prove that the objective was democratically authorised merely because the optimisation was technically impressive.

This distinction becomes increasingly important as technological systems acquire greater economic and social influence.

The ability to construct a future is not equivalent to acquiring ownership of the future.

