8. Externalities, Lawsuits and the Universal Mission

Claims of universal benefit become particularly interesting when the institutions making them simultaneously generate substantial negative externalities.

An organisation can declare a mission benefiting humanity while:

* fighting workers;
* upsetting customers;
* generating environmental costs;
* being sued;
* lobbying governments;
* resisting regulation;
* collecting enormous quantities of data;
* concentrating economic power;
* ignoring public criticism;
* and continuing to explain that all of this is necessary because the long-term benefits will be extraordinary.

This does not logically prove that the organisation’s technology is harmful.

It does, however, create a fascinating philosophical arrangement in which the people currently experiencing the supposed benefits are sometimes required to wait for a later version of humanity to appreciate them properly.

We call this Deferred Universal Gratitude.

