9. The Radicalisation of Certainty

The most dangerous element may not be greed.

Greed is comparatively easy to understand.

The more peculiar risk is certainty.

A person who knows they are pursuing personal profit can at least recognise competing interests.

A person convinced they are acting on behalf of humanity can rationalise almost anything.

Criticism becomes obstruction.

Regulation becomes backwardness.

Delay becomes dangerous.

Competitors become irresponsible.

Public hesitation becomes ignorance.

The mission grows morally larger precisely as its decision-making constituency grows smaller.

At the extreme, a tiny group of people may become completely convinced that humanity’s future depends upon humanity not interfering with them.

Historically, this has not always been an encouraging sentence structure.

