11. Conclusion

This paper does not establish that technological progress is undesirable.

It establishes something considerably more troublesome:

Nobody knows what “humanity wants,” because humanity is not a person.

Humanity has no CEO.

It has no mission statement.

It has no unified utility function.

It has issued no strategic roadmap.

It has not appointed a representative to the board.

Consequently, the statement:

“I am building this because I believe it will benefit humanity.”

is perfectly coherent.

The statement:

“I am building this for humanity.”

contains a political claim hiding inside a technological one.

The distinction matters.

You are free to build your machine.

You are free to believe in it.

You are free to argue that it will improve the world.

You may even be right.

But if you intend to claim eight billion beneficiaries as co-signatories to your mission, there remains one inconvenient administrative detail:

Humanity was not consulted.

Until it is, “for all humanity” should perhaps be replaced with the scientifically more defensible:

“For what a relatively small number of us currently believe would probably be good for everybody else.”

It lacks grandeur.

It does, however, have substantially better methodology.
